# phrase macros for question prompts: %name% expands to a random member at
# render time. the point is not decoration -- it is that the SAME skill is
# asked many different ways, so you read for intent instead of pattern-matching
# the sentence. every member of a group must be grammatically interchangeable
# wherever the macro is used.
#
# format:  %name% = one | two | three
# keep members boringly interchangeable; cleverness that fits one sentence only
# belongs inline as {a|b|c} in that one question.

# --- put something on the screen ---
# "write out" and "print out" are gone on purpose: they mean writing to a file
# or to paper, and every question here asks for something on the screen. they
# read as an instruction to redirect -- exactly the wrong thing to teach.
show = print | show | display | output

# --- enumerate directory-ish contents ---
list = list | show | show me

# --- locate something ---
find = find | locate | hunt down | track down | search for | dig out

# --- produce a count ---
count = count | tally | total up | add up

# --- pull a piece out of something ---
get = extract | pull out | grab | get | isolate | pick out | carve out

# --- open an archive. its own group: "carve out site-backup.tar.gz into build"
# is not something anyone would say, and %get% is for a field in a line ---
unpack = extract | unpack

# --- create ---
make = make | create | set up | build | spin up

# --- change something into something ---
turn = turn | convert | change

# --- polite openers (can prefix an instruction) ---
give = give me | show me | get me | i want | i need | hand me

# --- soft closers / flavor (optional, includes empty) ---
now = | right now | here | for me | quickly
